    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-129399",
      "knowledge_point_id": "85388f7d-b2ee-520b-b711-a7721c6f620c",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Variance of a Transformed Random Variable",
      "problem": "The probability distribution of the random variable $X$ is given below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{4},$ calculate $\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}-4X].$ \n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that for any random variable $X$ and constants $a$ and $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+b]={a}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=-4$ and $b=\\sqrt{5}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}-4X] & ={(-4)}^{2}\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\text{Var}[X].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the variance of $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nAdditionally, we're given that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{4}.$ \n\nWe start by computing $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={0}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{1}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{2}^{2}\\cdot f(2) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{3}{8})+1(\\frac{1}{2})+4(\\frac{1}{8}) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =1-{(\\frac{3}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\sqrt{5}-4X] & =16\\cdot \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =16\\cdot \\frac{7}{16} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
